# Runtime parameters for the napl headless MAVSDK-only SITL flight test.
#
# Mechanism (verified in PX4 v1.17.0 ROMFS/px4fmu_common/init.d-posix/rcS): rcS loops over the environment and runs
# `param set <NAME> <value>` for every PX4_PARAM_<NAME>=<value> variable, after SYS_AUTOCONFIG's `param reset_all`
# and before the airframe file is sourced. So these are applied by exporting env vars before launching px4:
#
#   while read -r n v; do [ -n "$n" ] && export "PX4_PARAM_$n=$v"; done < <(grep -v '^[[:space:]]*#' px4/params/napl_sitl.params)
#
# Format: one `NAME VALUE` per line, '#' comment lines only (no trailing comments). Use a FRESH working dir per run
# (parameters.bson there persists values across runs).

# Battery comes from our simulation via MAVLink BATTERY_STATUS; disable PX4's built-in linear battery_simulator.
SIM_BAT_ENABLE 0
# No RC in the test: 4 = disable manual control input entirely (rcS default is 1, MAVLink-only, which still expects stick messages).
COM_RC_IN_MODE 4
# No data-link-loss failsafe (0 = disabled; already the default, pinned so a PX4 default change cannot affect the test).
NAV_DLL_ACT 0

# ---- navigation quality (N5c; evidence: tools/px4_nav_diag.py, docs/research/px4-sitl.md "N5c") ----
# Barometer auto-calibration OFF. Root cause of the +-2..3 m height jumps: sensors/vehicle_air_data VehicleAirData::BaroGNSSAltitudeOffset
# (SENS_BAR_AUTOCAL, default 1) shifts the baro pressure ~4 s after the first GNSS fix so that the baro altitude equals the GNSS altitude
# averaged over 2 s. Our GNSS vertical error is 3.2 m (1 sigma, datasheet-derived), our MS5611 model has no offset at all (bias 0; its ISA
# pressure is referenced to the same origin_alt as the GNSS), so the calibration can only import GNSS error into the baro: observed jump
# +1.95 m at t = 5 s on the ground, after the EKF had already started, which moved the local down state by -1.9 m (reported "down +3.19 m on
# the ground"), moved the home altitude, and made PX4's relative / AMSL altitudes (RTL altitude, takeoff target) disagree by metres.
# With it off the baro altitude error is constant 0.30 m (= the Pixhawk site height above the base body) with 0.06 m noise.
SENS_BAR_AUTOCAL 0
# Height reference = barometer (0); the v1.17.0 default is GPS (1). GNSS vertical error (3.2 m, 5 Hz) is 30x the MS5611 model's (1.2 Pa =
# 0.1 m): measured with the baro-autocal off, GPS reference -> truth hold altitude 6.4-6.6 m for a 5 m target (GNSS error leaks into the
# height state), baro reference -> 4.9-5.0 m. The GNSS altitude still initialises the baro bias, and (EKF2_GPS_CTRL default 7) is fused
# against the baro reference, so the reported AMSL altitude follows the GNSS bias as on a real vehicle.
EKF2_HGT_REF 0
# EKF assumed baro noise (default 3.5 m, sized for a real quad: prop wash, gusts). The device model has only the MS5611 RMS noise,
# 1.2 Pa (OSR 4096 datasheet) / (rho g = 1.17 kg/m3 x 9.81) = 0.105 m; 0.3 m keeps ~3x margin for quantisation and ground effect.
EKF2_BARO_NOISE 0.3
# GNSS delay. Our receiver model outputs the sample taken round(latency_s x rate_hz) samples ago: latency_s 0.2 s (assembly and fixture),
# plus one 1 ms tick before a sample becomes visible; PX4's default is 110 ms. 200 ms makes the EKF fuse each fix at the time it
# describes: the mismatch (90 ms x 3 m/s = 0.27 m of position bias in the square) was visible as 0.18 m fast / 2.1 m max error before.
EKF2_GPS_DELAY 200
